[{"location":"jcc/#Joint-probability-functions","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"Joint probability functions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"jcc/#Computing-probability-functions","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"Computing probability functions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"jcc/","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"Joint Chance Contraints","text":"compute_with_QMC","category":"page"},{"location":"jcc/#ChanceConstraints.compute_with_QMC","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"ChanceConstraints.compute_with_QMC","text":"    compute_with_QMC(j, κ, Σ, μ; two_sided=false, s=10_000, rng=MersenneTwister(1234))\n\nCompute a quasi-Monte Carlo (QMC) approximation of a joint probability and its gradient.\n\nLet xi be a random vector with mean mu and covariance matrix Sigma. For the indices i = j ldots j+kappa, the one-sided probability is\n\nf(x) = mathbbPbigl(x_i geq xi_i i = j ldots j+kappabigr)\n\nWhen two_sided=true, the probability is computed using the corresponding two-sided inequalities.\n\nReturns (f, ∇f), where f is the estimated probability and ∇f is its gradient with respect to x. The gradient is computed using the Prékopa theorem, as described in Section X.\n\nArguments\n\nj::Integer: Starting index of the components in Sigma and mu.\nκ::Integer: Number of additional components. The joint event includes indices j ldots j+kappa.\nΣ::AbstractMatrix: Covariance matrix of xi.\nμ::AbstractVector: Mean vector of xi.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\ntwo_sided::Bool=false: Whether to use two-sided inequalities.\ns::Integer=10_000: Number of QMC samples.\nrng=MersenneTwister(1234): Random number generator for sampling. The fixed default seed makes estimates repeatable across solver iterations, which can help optimization converge. For standalone probability evaluation with a non-fixed random source, pass RandomDevice().\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"jcc/","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"Joint Chance Contraints","text":"compute_with_SRD","category":"page"},{"location":"jcc/#ChanceConstraints.compute_with_SRD","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"ChanceConstraints.compute_with_SRD","text":"    compute_with_SRD(w, κ, transformed_samples, μ)\n\nCompute a spherical radial decomposition (SRD) approximation of a joint probability and its gradient.\n\nFor the indices i = w ldots w+kappa, the probability is\n\nf(x) = mathbbPbigl(x_i geq xi_i i = w ldots w+kappabigr)\n\nwhere the random-variable samples used in the SRD calculation are provided by transformed_samples, and mu is the mean vector.\n\nReturns (f, ∇f), where f is the estimated probability and ∇f is its gradient with respect to x. The gradient is computed using the Prékopa theorem, as described in Section X.\n\nArguments\n\nw::Integer: Starting index of the components in the joint event.\nκ::Integer: Number of additional components in the event; the event covers indices w ldots w+kappa.\ntransformed_samples::AbstractMatrix: Transformed samples used by the SRD calculation.\nμ::AbstractVector: Mean vector used in the probability calculation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"jcc/#Adding-chance-constraints-to-a-JuMP-model","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"Adding chance constraints to a JuMP model","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"jcc/","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"Joint Chance Contraints","text":"add_JCC_QMC","category":"page"},{"location":"jcc/#ChanceConstraints.add_JCC_QMC","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"ChanceConstraints.add_JCC_QMC","text":"    add_JCC_QMC(m, x, idx, κ, Σ, μ, p; two_sided=false, s=10_000, rng=MersenneTwister(1234))\n\nAdd a system of joint chance constraints to a JuMP model.\n\nFor each starting index j in textttidx, the added constraint is\n\nmathbbPbigl(x_i geq xi_i i = j ldots j+kappabigr) geq p\n\nwhere xi is a random vector with mean mu and covariance matrix Sigma. When two_sided=true, the probability uses the corresponding two-sided inequalities.\n\nThe probability is approximated using quasi-Monte Carlo (QMC). The function registers it as a user-defined operator in the JuMP model and supplies the gradient computed by compute_with_QMC.\n\nArguments\n\nm::JuMP.Model: JuMP model to which the constraints are added.\nx::AbstractVector: Decision variables used in the constraints.\nidx::AbstractArray: Starting indices of the reliability windows.\nκ::Integer: Number of additional components in each window; each probability covers indices j ldots j+kappa.\nΣ::AbstractMatrix: Covariance matrix of xi.\nμ::AbstractArray: Mean vector of xi.\np::Float64: Minimum required probability level.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\ntwo_sided::Bool=false: Whether to use two-sided inequalities.\ns::Integer=10_000: Number of QMC samples.\nrng=MersenneTwister(1234): Random number generator for QMC sampling. The fixed default seed makes estimates repeatable across solver iterations, which can help optimization converge. For standalone probability evaluation with a non-fixed random source, pass RandomDevice().\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"jcc/","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"Joint Chance Contraints","text":"add_JCC_SRD","category":"page"},{"location":"jcc/#ChanceConstraints.add_JCC_SRD","page":"Joint Chance Contraints","title":"ChanceConstraints.add_JCC_SRD","text":"    add_JCC_SRD(model, x, idx, κ, Σ, μ, p; s=100_000, seed=1234)\n\nAdd a system of joint chance constraints to a JuMP model using spherical radial decomposition (SRD).\n\nFor each starting index j in textttidx, the added constraint is\n\nmathbbPbigl(x_i geq xi_i i = j ldots j+kappabigr) geq p\n\nwhere xi is a random vector with mean mu and covariance matrix Sigma.\n\nThe probability is computed using compute_with_SRD. The function registers it as a user-defined operator in the JuMP model and supplies its gradient, also computed using compute_with_SRD.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::JuMP.Model: JuMP model to which the constraints are added.\nx::AbstractVector: Decision variables used in the constraints.\nidx::AbstractVector: Starting indices of the reliability windows.\nκ::Integer: Number of additional components in each window; each probability covers indices j ldots j+kappa.\nΣ::AbstractMatrix: Covariance matrix of xi.\nμ::AbstractVector: Mean vector of xi.\np::Real: Minimum required probability level.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\ns::Integer=100_000: Number of samples used by the SRD computation.\nseed::Integer=1234: Seed used for the random-number generation. The default makes the estimates repeatable across solver iterations, which can help optimization converge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"This page shows ChanceConstraints.jl in use, from minimal examples to a full comparison against alternative formulations using deterministic and individual chance constraints models.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Example What it shows Files\nSimpleExample Minimal setup and solve SimpleExample.jl\nReservoir problem Simple reservoir problem formulation and solve ReservoirExample.jl\nFinancial portfolio optimization Full comparison between deterministic vs. individual vs. joint chance constraints on a realistic problem FinancialPortfolio/","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/#Simple-example","page":"Examples","title":"Simple example","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"A joint chance constraint requires that all components of a random vector are satisfied simultaneously with probability at least p. This example illustrates the minimal workflow for that case.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Problem. Find the cheapest x with unit costs c such that","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"mathbbP(xi le x) ge p qquad xi sim mathcalN(mu Sigma) quad p = 09","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"The covariance Σ has non-zero off-diagonal entries, so the components are correlated and the joint probability cannot be split into independent per-component constraints.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"What the example shows.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Building a JuMP model and adding the joint chance constraint with  add_JCC_QMC, which uses quasi-Monte Carlo to approximate the probability.\nSolving the same problem with add_JCC_SRD, which uses spherical-radial decomposition.\nVerifying the optimal solution with an independent estimate of P(ξ ≤ x*), which should be close to p.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Both formulations should give the same optimal cost.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"using Markdown\ncode = read(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"..\", \"examples\", \"SimpleExample.jl\"), String)\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * code * \"\\n```\")","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/#Reservoir-problem-example","page":"Examples","title":"Reservoir problem example","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/#Joint-chance-constrained-formulation","page":"Examples","title":"Joint-chance-constrained formulation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"A hydropower operator chooses how much water to release from a reservoir in each of T periods. Releases generate revenue, but the water level must stay within safe limits in every period. Because inflows are uncertain, the requirement is stated as a joint chance constraint: the level must stay within its bounds in all periods simultaneously with probability at least p.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/#Model","page":"Examples","title":"Model","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Let x[t] be the release in period t and ξ[t] the cumulative inflow up to period t. The water level is","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"ell_t = ell_0 + xi_t - sum_s=1^t x_s ","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"The cumulative inflow is ξ ~ N(μ, Σ). It is built from independent yearly inflows with means β_t and variances σ²_t, so","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"mu_t = sum_s=1^t beta_s qquad\nSigma_ij = sum_s=1^min(ij) sigma^2_s ","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"The cumulative sums make the levels strongly correlated across periods, which is why the constraints must be treated jointly. ","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"The optimization problem is","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"max_x  sum_t=1^T x_t\nquad textst quad\nmathbbPbig(ell_min le ell_t le ell_max t = 1dotsTbig) ge p\nqquad x_min le x_t le x_max ","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/#Writing-it-as-a-joint-chance-constraint","page":"Examples","title":"Writing it as a joint chance constraint","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"add_JCC_QMC and add_JCC_SRD handle constraints of the form P(ζ ≤ x) ≥ p with ζ ~ N(μ, Σ), so the level constraints are rewritten in that form.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Single-sided (lower bound only). The constraint ℓ_t ≥ ℓ_min is equivalent to","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"-xi_t le ell_0 - ell_min - sum_s le t x_s ","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"and -ξ ~ N(-μ, Σ). Pass the right-hand side (the slack) as the bound and -μ as the mean:","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"cumx  = [sum(x[1:t]) for t in 1:T]\nslack = [l_0 - l_min - cumx[t] for t in 1:T]\nadd_JCC_QMC(model, slack, [1], T - 1, Σ, -μ, p)","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"The window starting at time step [1] with κ = T - 1 covers all T periods in a single joint constraint.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Two-sided (lower and upper bound). The constraint ℓ_min ≤ ℓ_t ≤ ℓ_max is equivalent to lower_t ≤ ξ_t ≤ upper_t with","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"textlower_t = ell_min - ell_0 + sum_s le t x_s qquad\ntextupper_t = ell_max - ell_0 + sum_s le t x_s ","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Stack the bounds as [lower; upper] (length 2T) and set two_sided = true. The random vector still has T components, so κ stays T - 1:","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"add_JCC_QMC(model, vcat(lower, upper), [1], T - 1, Σ, μ, p; two_sided = true)","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/#What-the-example-shows","page":"Examples","title":"What the example shows","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Single-sided, QMC and SRD. Both approximations give the same optimal total release.\nTwo-sided, QMC. Adding the upper bound makes the problem slightly more restrictive. Two-sided implementation is currently not possible using SRD.\nMonte Carlo check. The probability that the level stays within bounds is estimated by simulating 200,000 inflow scenarios. It should be close to p = 0.9 for both solutions.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"using Markdown\ncode = read(joinpath(pwd(), \"..\", \"..\", \"examples\", \"ReservoirExample.jl\"), String)\nMarkdown.parse(\"```julia\\n\" * code * \"\\n```\")","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/#Individual-chance-constrained-formulation","page":"Examples","title":"Individual-chance-constrained formulation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Instead of requiring the level to stay in bounds in all periods jointly, each period is constrained on its own:","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"mathbbP(ell_t ge ell_min) ge p qquad t = 1dotsT ","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"This ignores the correlation between periods. Because each ξ_t is a scalar Gaussian, ξ_t ~ N(μ_t, Σ_tt), every constraint has a closed form. With z_p = Φ⁻¹(p) and σ_t = √Σ_tt:","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"mathbbP(-xi_t le ell_0 - ell_min - textstylesum_s le t x_s) ge p\niff\nsum_s le t x_s le ell_0 - ell_min + mu_t - z_psigma_t ","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"These are linear in x, so no package call is needed:","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"using Distributions, LinearAlgebra\n\nz = quantile(Normal(), p)\nσ = sqrt.(diag(Σ))\n\nmodel = Model(Ipopt.Optimizer)\nset_silent(model)\n\n@variable(model, 0 <= x[1:T] <= 30)\n@objective(model, Max, sum(x))\n\ncumx = [sum(x[1:t]) for t in 1:T]\n@constraint(model, [t = 1:T], cumx[t] <= l_0 - l_min + μ[t] - z * σ[t])\n\noptimize!(model)\nx_ind = value.(x)\nobjective_value(model)","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Two-sided version. The constraint P(lower_t ≤ ξ_t ≤ upper_t) ≥ p becomes","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Phileft(tfractextupper_t - mu_tsigma_tright)\n- Phileft(tfractextlower_t - mu_tsigma_tright) ge p ","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"which is nonlinear but smooth. Using Φ(u) = ½(1 + erf(u/√2)):","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"using SpecialFunctions\n\nΦ(u) = 0.5 * (1 + erf(u / sqrt(2)))\n\nlower = [l_min - l_0 + cumx[t] for t in 1:T]\nupper = [l_max - l_0 + cumx[t] for t in 1:T]\n\n@constraint(model, [t = 1:T],\n    Φ((upper[t] - μ[t]) / σ[t]) - Φ((lower[t] - μ[t]) / σ[t]) >= p)","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Checking the joint probability. Reuse the Monte Carlo check from the joint example on x_ind:","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"mean(all(level(x_ind) .>= l_min; dims = 1))","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/","page":"Examples","title":"Examples","text":"Each period satisfies its own constraint with probability p, but the probability that all periods are satisfied together is generally lower than p. That gap is what the joint formulation closes.","category":"page"},{"location":"examples/#Financial-portfolio-example","page":"Examples","title":"Financial portfolio example","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"icc/#Individual-probability-functions","page":"Individual Chance Contraints","title":"Individual probability functions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"#JointChance.jl","page":"Home","title":"JointChance.jl","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"A package for dealing with Joint Chance Constraints in Julia.","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"index.md\"]","category":"page"},{"location":"#Install","page":"Home","title":"Install","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"# pkg> add \"https://github.com/naouess/JointChance\"","category":"page"},{"location":"#Resources-for-getting-started","page":"Home","title":"Resources for getting started","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Here some math stuff \nSome JuMP and Julia stuff \nRefer to our paper in Annals of OR","category":"page"},{"location":"#The-math-behind-it-all","page":"Home","title":"The math behind it all","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Here we explain Chance constraints ","category":"page"}]
